Here is the JavaScript code to sort an array of objects by a numeric field called `count`.

The `sort()` method accepts a comparator function. When sorting numbers, you must return the result of the subtraction (`a - b`) to ensure ascending order.

```javascript
// 1. Define the array of objects with the provided counts
const items = [
  { name: "Item A", count: 10 },
  { name: "Item B", count: 17 },
  { name: "Item C", count: 23 }
];

// 2. Sort the array in ascending order by 'count'
items.sort((a, b) => a.count - b.count);

// 3. Output the result
console.log(items);
```

### Output
When you run this code, the array will be arranged from the lowest count to the highest:

```javascript
[
  { name: "Item A", count: 10 },
  { name: "Item B", count: 17 },
  { name: "Item C", count: 23 }
]
```

### Note on Descending Order
If you need to sort from **highest to lowest** (descending), simply reverse the subtraction:

```javascript
items.sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count);
```
